<p class="bench-note">Both runtimes snapshot in O(objects) and stay flat as each actor grows larger (17 µs for 64-byte actors, 21.9 µs for 16384-byte actors). They share each actor's state instead of copying it. The runtimes differ, so their absolute times are not directly comparable; the shared O(objects) trend is the result. On restore, glowcap frees the rewound copies right away; Goblins leaves that cleanup to its garbage collector.</p>
```

# [Persistent actors in Loam]($section.id('persistent-actors-in-loam'))

[Loam]($link.page('loam')) keeps actors in a database and loads one only when a
call reaches it. `bench-loam` builds a graph of conversations in LMDB and grows
it from 64 to 2,048. A call to an asleep actor pays one record load. Each
conversation costs a small, fixed amount of Zig heap, far less than its records
on disk. A restart loads only the record its first call needs, though its time
still grows with the population. The
[workload notes](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/bench/loam/README.md)
describe each phase.

```=html
